{
    "map": {
        "description": "Creates 3D features by interpolating z-values from a surface.",
        "in_feature_class.descr": "The input features that will be processed.",
        "in_feature_class.title": "Input Features",
        "in_surface.descr": "The surface that will be used for interpolating z-values.",
        "in_surface.title": "Input Surface",
        "method.descr": "<xdoc><p>Specifies the interpolation method that will be used to determine elevation values for the output features. The available options depend on the surface type.</p><ul><li><span title=\"BILINEAR\">Bilinear</span>—The value of the query point will be determined using bilinear interpolation. This is the default when the input is a raster surface.</li><li><span title=\"NEAREST\">Nearest Neighbor</span>—The value of the query point will be determined using nearest neighbor interpolation. With this method, surface values will only be interpolated for the input feature&apos;s vertices. This option is only available for a raster surface.</li><li><span title=\"LINEAR\">Linear</span>—Elevation values will be obtained from the plane defined by the triangle that contains the x,y-location of a query point. This is the default interpolation method for TIN, terrain, and LAS datasets.</li><li><span title=\"NATURAL_NEIGHBORS\">Natural Neighbors</span>—Elevation values will be obtained by applying area-based weights to the natural neighbors of a query point.</li><li><span title=\"CONFLATE_ZMIN\">Conflate Minimum Z</span>— Elevation values will be obtained from the smallest z-value found among the natural neighbors of a query point.</li><li><span title=\"CONFLATE_ZMAX\">Conflate Maximum Z</span>—Elevation values will be obtained from the largest z-value found among the natural neighbors of a query point.</li><li><span title=\"CONFLATE_NEAREST\">Conflate Nearest Z</span>— Elevation values will be obtained from the nearest value among the natural neighbors of a query point.</li><li><span title=\"CONFLATE_CLOSEST_TO_MEAN\">Conflate Z Closest To Mean</span>— Elevation values will be obtained from the z-value that is closest to the average of all the natural neighbors of a query point.</li></ul></xdoc>",
        "method.domain.BILINEAR": "Bilinear",
        "method.domain.CONFLATE_CLOSEST_TO_MEAN": "Conflate Z Closest To Mean",
        "method.domain.CONFLATE_NEAREST": "Conflate Nearest Z",
        "method.domain.CONFLATE_ZMAX": "Conflate Maximum Z",
        "method.domain.CONFLATE_ZMIN": "Conflate Minimum Z",
        "method.domain.LINEAR": "Linear",
        "method.domain.NATURAL_NEIGHBORS": "Natural Neighbors",
        "method.domain.NEAREST": "Nearest Neighbor",
        "method.title": "Method",
        "out_feature_class.descr": "The feature class that will be produced.",
        "out_feature_class.title": "Output Feature Class",
        "preserve_features.descr": "<xdoc><p>Specifies whether features with one or more vertices that fall outside the raster&apos;s data area will be retained in the output. This parameter is only available when the input surface is a raster and the nearest neighbor interpolation method is used.</p><ul><li>Checked—Each vertex that falls outside the raster surface will have its z-value derived from the trend of z-values calculated for the vertices within the raster surface and will be retained in the output.</li><li>Unchecked—Features with at least one vertex that falls outside the raster surface will be skipped in the output. This is the default.</li></ul></xdoc>",
        "preserve_features.title": "Preserve features partially outside surface",
        "pyramid_level_resolution.descr": "The z-tolerance or window-size resolution of the terrain pyramid level that will be used. The default is 0, or full resolution.",
        "pyramid_level_resolution.title": "Pyramid Level Resolution",
        "sample_distance.descr": "The spacing at which z-values will be interpolated. By default, this is a raster dataset's cell size or a triangulated surface's natural densification.",
        "sample_distance.title": "Sampling Distance",
        "summary": "<xdoc><p>Creates 3D features by interpolating z-values from a surface.</p><p>Learn more about how Interpolate Shape works</p></xdoc>",
        "title": "Interpolate Shape",
        "vertices_only.descr": "<xdoc><p>Specifies whether the interpolation will only occur along the vertices of an input feature, ignoring the sample distance option. When the input surface is a raster and the nearest neighbor interpolation method is selected, the z-values can only be interpolated at the feature vertices.</p><ul><li>Checked—Interpolation will only occur along the vertices.</li><li>Unchecked—Interpolation will occur using the sampling distance. This is the default.</li></ul></xdoc>",
        "vertices_only.title": "Interpolate Vertices Only",
        "z_factor.descr": "The factor by which z-values will be multiplied. This is typically used to convert z linear units to match x,y linear units. The default is 1, which leaves elevation values unchanged. This parameter is not available if the spatial reference of the input surface has a z-datum with a specified linear unit.",
        "z_factor.title": "Z Factor"
    }
}
